    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d148">

                <pb facs="0242" n="162" xml:id="pg162"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">500.A15A5 Submarines/4: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Acting Secrtary of State
                        to the Chairman of the American Delegation to the London Naval
                        Conference</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Davis</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1936-03-20T20:00:00-05:00">March 20, 1936—8
                            p.m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>76. Your 143, March 19, 1 p.m.<anchor n="4" xml:id="d148-f1"/> The President
                    authorizes the American delegates to the London Naval Conference, 1935, to sign
                    a procès-verbal designed to obtain further accessions to the rules of
                    international law concerning submarine warfare to which the United States as
                    well as the United Kingdom and Japan are now expressly committed under the
                    London Naval Treaty of 1930.<anchor n="5" xml:id="d148-f2"/>
                </p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">William Phillips</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d148-f1" n="4" xml:id="d148-f1-content">
                <p>Not printed.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d148-f2" n="5" xml:id="d148-f2-content">
                <p>The Department was informed by telegram No. 165, March 25, 10 p.m., from Mr.
                    Davis that the Japanese would not receive instructions in time for them to sign
                    the procès-verbal before the following week (500.A15A5 Submarines/11).</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
